343 10.7783C4.34386 10.5462 4.58164 10.312 4.90538 9.98829L11.1964 3.6963L11.3312 3.56837ZM5.84581 10.9287C5.49664 11.2779 5.31252 11.4634 5.18663 11.6162L5.07531 11.7627C4.98188 11.8995 4.90151 12.0448 4.83507 12.1963L4.77355 12.3506C4.73321 12.4607 4.70242 12.5761 4.66808 12.7451L4.54113 13.4619L4.14269 15.8555L4.14171 15.8574H4.14464L6.5382 15.458L7.25499 15.332C7.424 15.2977 7.5394 15.2669 7.64953 15.2266L7.80285 15.165C7.95455 15.0986 8.09947 15.0174 8.23644 14.9238L8.3839 14.8135C8.53668 14.6876 8.72225 14.5035 9.0714 14.1543L14.0587 9.16602L10.8331 5.94044L5.84581 10.9287ZM15.3634 4.63673C14.5281 3.80141 13.2057 3.74938 12.3097 4.48048L12.1368 4.63673L11.7735 5.00001L15.0001 8.22559L15.3634 7.86329L15.5196 7.68946C16.2015 6.85326 16.2015 5.64676 15.5196 4.81056L15.3634 4.63673Z"></path></svg></span></button></div></div></div></div></article><article class="text-token-text-primary w-full focus:outline-none scroll-mt-[calc(var(--header-height)+min(200px,max(70px,20svh)))]" tabindex="-1" dir="auto" data-turn-id="2e896229-07e9-4879-b7ad-6ff3682546ae" data-testid="conversation-turn-4" data-scroll-anchor="false" data-turn="assistant"><h6 class="sr-only">ChatGPT said:</h6><div class="text-base my-auto mx-auto [--thread-content-margin:--spacing(4)] thread-sm:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(6)] thread-lg:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(16)] px-(--thread-content-margin)"><div class="[--thread-content-max-width:40rem] thread-lg:[--thread-content-max-width:48rem] mx-auto max-w-(--thread-content-max-width) flex-1 group/turn-messages focus-visible:outline-hidden relative flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col agent-turn" tabindex="-1"><div class="flex max-w-full flex-col grow"><div data-message-author-role="assistant" data-message-id="2e896229-07e9-4879-b7ad-6ff3682546ae" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-5" data-message-model-slug="gpt-5"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[3px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="129">That’s a fascinating snapshot of how early computing was framed in the 1950s. A few things stand out from the excerpt you shared:</p>
<hr data-start="131" data-end="134">
<h3 data-start="136" data-end="180">1. Emergence of a Computing Literature</h3>
<p data-start="181" data-end="270">By the mid-1950s, computing had already begun to generate its own periodical ecosystem:</p>
<ul data-start="271" data-end="474">
<li data-start="271" data-end="330">
<p data-start="273" data-end="330"><strong data-start="273" data-end="318">Association for Computing Machinery (ACM)</strong> journals.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="331" data-end="393">
<p data-start="333" data-end="393"><strong data-start="333" data-end="382">Data Processing Management Association (DPMA)</strong> outlets.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="394" data-end="474">
<p data-start="396" data-end="474">Private magazines like <em data-start="419" data-end="440">Business Automation</em> and <em data-start="445" data-end="471">Computers and Automation</em>.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="476" data-end="739">The text is emphasizing that computing wasn’t just an engineering niche—it was spilling into accounting, psychology, sales management, and other applied fields. That diffusion is what turned computers from scientific curiosities into institutional infrastructure.</p>
<hr data-start="741" data-end="744">
<h3 data-start="746" data-end="781">2. Accessibility of Knowledge</h3>
<p data-start="782" data-end="1133">The author notes that <strong data-start="804" data-end="849">journals were harder to obtain than books</strong>—which makes sense in the pre-digital era when interlibrary loan and indexing were limited. That’s why the “Suggested Readings” stuck mostly to monographs. It also reflects how <strong data-start="1026" data-end="1075">books were trusted as authoritative syntheses</strong>, while periodicals were seen as scattered or provisional.</p>
<hr data-start="1135" data-end="1138">
<h3 data-start="1140" data-end="1178">3. “Can Computers Think?” Debate</h3>
<p data-start="1179" data-end="1227">The text references the famous back-and-forth:</p>
<ul data-start="1228" data-end="1607">
<li data-start="1228" data-end="1346">
<p data-start="1230" data-end="1346"><strong data-start="1230" data-end="1276">Edmund C. Berkeley’s <em data-start="1253" data-end="1267">Giant Brains</em> (1949)</strong>, which cast computers as machines that could “think” in some sense.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1347" data-end="1607">
<p data-start="1349" data-end="1607"><strong data-start="1349" data-end="1389">Alan Turing’s essay (reprinted 1956)</strong>, which—despite the citation—was not titled <em data-start="1433" data-end="1455">Can a Machine Think?</em> but rather “Computing Machinery and Intelligence” (1950). It seems the editor referenced the version included in Newman’s <em data-start="1578" data-end="1604">The World of Mathematics</em>.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="1609" data-end="1724">The aside that “Mr. Berkeley has since modified his views” already hints at how quickly perspectives were evolving.</p>
<hr data-start="1726" data-end="1729">
<h3 data-start="1731" data-end="1758">4. Introductory Canon</h3>
<p data-start="1759" data-end="2179">The list of suggested readings—Gregory &amp; Van Horn, Hein, Nett &amp; Hetzler, Williams—shows what counted as “entry points” for business and technical students. These were not speculative AI books but <strong data-start="1955" data-end="2004">manuals for data processing and system design</strong>. It shows the practical orientation of computing education at the time: more about how to <em data-start="2095" data-end="2100">use</em> machines for accounting, business, and engineering than about abstract theory.</p>
<hr data-start="2181" data-end="2184">
<h3 data-start="2186" data-end="2218">5. Historical Significance</h3>
<p data-start="2219" data-end="2265">This passage captures a transitional moment:</p>
<ul data-start="2266" data-end="2449">
<li data-start="2266" data-end="2318">
<p data-start="2268" data-end="2318">Computing was <strong data-start="2282" data-end="2303">professionalizing</strong> (ACM, DPMA).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2319" data-end="2389">
<p data-start="2321" data-end="2389">Computing was <strong data-start="2335" data-end="2354">commercializing</strong> (business automation magazines).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2390" data-end="2449">
<p data-start="2392" data-end="2449">Computing was <strong data-start="2406" data-end="2424">philosophizing</strong> (Berkeley vs. Turing).</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="2451" data-end="2614">The text stands at the threshold between computers as <strong data-start="2505" data-end="2540">specialized calculating engines</strong> and computers as <strong data-start="2558" data-end="2613">general cognitive and organizational infrastructure</strong>.</p>
<hr data-start="2616" data-end="2619">
<p data-start="2621" data-end="2896" data-is-last-node="" data-is-only-node="">Would you like me to put this into a <strong data-start="2658" data-end="2701">modernized annotated bibliography style</strong> (mapping each cited source to its role in early computing thought), or into a <strong data-start="2780" data-end="2815">historical commentary narrative</strong> that situates this excerpt within the broader timeline of computing’s emergence?</p></div></div></div></div><div class="flex min-h-[46px] justify-start"><div class="touch:-me-2 touch:-ms-3.5 -ms-2.5 -me-1 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-y-4 p-1 select-none touch:w-[calc(100%+--spacing(3.5))] -mt-1 w-[calc(100%+--spacing(2.5))] duration-[1.5s] focus-within:transition-none hover:transition-none pointer-events-none [mask-image:linear-gradient(to_right,black_33%,transparent_66%)] [mask-size:300%_100%] [mask-position:100%_0%] motion-safe:transition-[mask-position] group-hover/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-hover/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] group-focus-within/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-focus-within/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] has-data-[state=open]:pointer-events-auto has-data-[state=open]:[mask-position:0_0]" style="mask-position: 0% 0%;"><button class="text-token-text-secondary hover:bg-token-bg-secondary rounded-lg" aria-label="Copy" aria-pressed="false" data-testid="copy-turn-action-button" data-state="closed"><span class="flex items-center justify-center touch:w-10 h-8 w-8"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="currentColor" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="icon"><path d="M12.668 10.667C12.668 9.95614 12.668 9.46258 12.6367 9.0791C12.6137 8.79732 12.5758 8.60761 12.5244 8.46387L12.4688 8.33399C12.3148 8.03193 12.0803 7.77885 11.793 7.60254L11.666 7.53125C11.508 7.45087 11.2963 7.39395 10.9209 7.36328C10.5374 7.33197 10.0439 7.33203 9.33301 7.33203H6.5C5.78896 7.33203 5.29563 7.33195 4.91211 7.36328C4.63016 7.38632 4.44065 7.42413 4.29688 7.47559L4.1