1.6621C3.71685 11.4352 3.83766 11.2176 3.97765 11.0127L4.15343 10.7783C4.34386 10.5462 4.58164 10.312 4.90538 9.98829L11.1964 3.6963L11.3312 3.56837ZM5.84581 10.9287C5.49664 11.2779 5.31252 11.4634 5.18663 11.6162L5.07531 11.7627C4.98188 11.8995 4.90151 12.0448 4.83507 12.1963L4.77355 12.3506C4.73321 12.4607 4.70242 12.5761 4.66808 12.7451L4.54113 13.4619L4.14269 15.8555L4.14171 15.8574H4.14464L6.5382 15.458L7.25499 15.332C7.424 15.2977 7.5394 15.2669 7.64953 15.2266L7.80285 15.165C7.95455 15.0986 8.09947 15.0174 8.23644 14.9238L8.3839 14.8135C8.53668 14.6876 8.72225 14.5035 9.0714 14.1543L14.0587 9.16602L10.8331 5.94044L5.84581 10.9287ZM15.3634 4.63673C14.5281 3.80141 13.2057 3.74938 12.3097 4.48048L12.1368 4.63673L11.7735 5.00001L15.0001 8.22559L15.3634 7.86329L15.5196 7.68946C16.2015 6.85326 16.2015 5.64676 15.5196 4.81056L15.3634 4.63673Z"></path></svg></span></button></div></div></div></div></article><article class="text-token-text-primary w-full focus:outline-none scroll-mt-[calc(var(--header-height)+min(200px,max(70px,20svh)))]" tabindex="-1" dir="auto" data-turn-id="f33f297e-7af5-4863-9e87-f2d0a0a1dd75" data-testid="conversation-turn-6" data-scroll-anchor="false" data-turn="assistant"><h6 class="sr-only">ChatGPT said:</h6><div class="text-base my-auto mx-auto [--thread-content-margin:--spacing(4)] thread-sm:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(6)] thread-lg:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(16)] px-(--thread-content-margin)"><div class="[--thread-content-max-width:40rem] thread-lg:[--thread-content-max-width:48rem] mx-auto max-w-(--thread-content-max-width) flex-1 group/turn-messages focus-visible:outline-hidden relative flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col agent-turn" tabindex="-1"><div class="flex max-w-full flex-col grow"><div data-message-author-role="assistant" data-message-id="f33f297e-7af5-4863-9e87-f2d0a0a1dd75" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-5" data-message-model-slug="gpt-5"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[3px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="257">Exactly — that continuity is striking. The <strong data-start="43" data-end="65">surface conditions</strong> have changed dramatically (from million-dollar mainframes in universities and corporations to pocket devices in everyone’s hands), but the <strong data-start="205" data-end="229">conceptual arguments</strong> are surprisingly similar:</p>
<hr data-start="259" data-end="262">
<h3 data-start="264" data-end="310">1. The Same Debates in Different Clothes</h3>
<ul data-start="311" data-end="550">
<li data-start="311" data-end="428">
<p data-start="313" data-end="428"><strong data-start="313" data-end="334">Then (1950s–60s):</strong> Can computers think? Are they just glorified calculators, or do they embody “intelligence”?</p>
</li>
<li data-start="429" data-end="550">
<p data-start="431" data-end="550"><strong data-start="431" data-end="451">Now (2020s–30s):</strong> Can LLMs “understand”? Are they merely stochastic parrots, or do they exhibit genuine reasoning?</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="552" data-end="660">The words have changed — “giant brains” vs. “AGI” — but the structure of the argument is almost identical.</p>
<hr data-start="662" data-end="665">
<h3 data-start="667" data-end="704">2. From Niche to Infrastructure</h3>
<p data-start="705" data-end="1082">In 1955, the computer was <strong data-start="731" data-end="774">scarce, institutional, and experimental</strong>: only a few universities, government labs, and corporations could justify them. By today, computing is <strong data-start="878" data-end="907">ubiquitous infrastructure</strong>: phones, cars, appliances, schools, jobs — even relationships — assume digital mediation. What was once optional expertise has become <strong data-start="1042" data-end="1079">baseline participation in society</strong>.</p>
<hr data-start="1084" data-end="1087">
<h3 data-start="1089" data-end="1134">3. Perennial Anxiety About Substitution</h3>
<ul data-start="1135" data-end="1340">
<li data-start="1135" data-end="1244">
<p data-start="1137" data-end="1244">In the 1950s, debates circled around <em data-start="1174" data-end="1242">“Will computers replace human clerks, accountants, and engineers?”</em></p>
</li>
<li data-start="1245" data-end="1340">
<p data-start="1247" data-end="1340">Today, the discourse repeats with <em data-start="1281" data-end="1338">“Will AI replace programmers, artists, and scientists?”</em></p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="1342" data-end="1454">The boundary of “uniquely human work” is constantly renegotiated, but the underlying anxiety remains constant.</p>
<hr data-start="1456" data-end="1459">
<h3 data-start="1461" data-end="1491">4. Epistemic Bottlenecks</h3>
<p data-start="1492" data-end="1872">That 1955 book notes how <strong data-start="1517" data-end="1551">access to periodicals was hard</strong>, so readers were steered toward books. Now, the problem is inverted: periodicals, blogs, preprints, and feeds are <strong data-start="1666" data-end="1681">so abundant</strong> that coherence and trust are the scarce commodities. Then, too little access; now, too much noise. In both eras, the human challenge is not “raw computation” but <strong data-start="1844" data-end="1869">curation of knowledge</strong>.</p>
<hr data-start="1874" data-end="1877">
<h3 data-start="1879" data-end="1918">5. From Speculation to Dependency</h3>
<p data-start="1919" data-end="2325">Back then, computers were things one speculated about — “can they think, can they help us?”<br data-start="2010" data-end="2013">
Now, computers are things one <strong data-start="2043" data-end="2066">cannot live without</strong> — banking, healthcare, education, communication. The “speculative dialogue” has barely evolved, but the <strong data-start="2171" data-end="2198">stakes have intensified</strong>: a bad definition of “thinking machine” in 1955 was academic; a bad definition of AI in 2025 is a global governance problem.</p>
<hr data-start="2327" data-end="2330">
<p data-start="2332" data-end="2579" data-is-last-node="" data-is-only-node="">Would you like me to sketch a <strong data-start="2362" data-end="2387">side-by-side timeline</strong> of recurring themes (1950s vs. 2020s) to make the parallels clearer, or would you prefer a <strong data-start="2479" data-end="2498">long-form essay</strong> about why intellectual debates stagnate even as material dependence accelerates?</p></div></div></div></div><div class="flex min-h-[46px] justify-start"><div class="touch:-me-2 touch:-ms-3.5 -ms-2.5 -me-1 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-y-4 p-1 select-none touch:w-[calc(100%+--spacing(3.5))] -mt-1 w-[calc(100%+--spacing(2.5))] duration-[1.5s] focus-within:transition-none hover:transition-none pointer-events-none [mask-image:linear-gradient(to_right,black_33%,transparent_66%)] [mask-size:300%_100%] [mask-position:100%_0%] motion-safe:transition-[mask-position] group-hover/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-hover/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] group-focus-within/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-focus-within/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] has-data-[state=open]:pointer-events-auto has-data-[state=open]:[mask-position:0_0]" style="mask-position: 0% 0%;"><button class="text-token-text-secondary hover:bg-token-bg-secondary rounded-lg" aria-label="Copy" aria-pressed="false" data-testid="copy-turn-action-button" data-state="closed"><span class="flex items-center justify-center touch:w-10 h-8 w-8"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="currentColor" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="icon"><path d="M12.668 10.667C12.668 9.95614 12.668 9.46258 12.6367 9.0791C12.6137 8.79732 12.5758 8.60761 12.5244 8.46387L12.4688 8.33399C12.3148 8.03193 12.0803 7.77885 11.793 7.60254L11.666 7.53125C11.508 7.45087 11.2963 7.39395 10.9209 7.36328C10.5374 7.33197 10.0439 7.33203 9.33301 7.33203H6.5C5.78896 7.33203 5.29563 7.33195 4.91211 7.36328C4.63016 7.38632 4.44065 7.42413 4.29688 7.47559L4.16699 7.53125C3.86488 7.68518