"gpt-5"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[3px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="170">That passage is such a vivid period-piece. It’s doing the work of demystifying the “black box” idea of an <em data-start="106" data-end="126">automatic computer</em> in the mid-1950s. A few points stand out:</p>
<hr data-start="172" data-end="175">
<h3 data-start="177" data-end="219">1. The Myth of Effortless Automation</h3>
<p data-start="220" data-end="409">The author pushes back against the fantasy that you can just feed in “raw information” and get instant answers. Instead, they emphasize all the <strong data-start="364" data-end="397">human labor and preprocessing</strong> required:</p>
<ul data-start="410" data-end="594">
<li data-start="410" data-end="444">
<p data-start="412" data-end="444">Collecting and verifying data.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="445" data-end="519">
<p data-start="447" data-end="519">Preparing it in specific machine-readable formats (often punch cards).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="520" data-end="594">
<p data-start="522" data-end="594">Programming explicit instructions to get even a single “small report.”</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="596" data-end="792">That’s not so different from today, where people imagine an LLM prompt is “raw” — but it’s sitting atop billions of tokens of curated, preprocessed training data, plus careful model engineering.</p>
<hr data-start="794" data-end="797">
<h3 data-start="799" data-end="840">2. The Brain Analogy and Its Limits</h3>
<p data-start="841" data-end="962">Even in the 1950s, the comparison of computers to <strong data-start="891" data-end="901">brains</strong> was already common. The passage is careful to draw a line:</p>
<ul data-start="963" data-end="1153">
<li data-start="963" data-end="1058">
<p data-start="965" data-end="1058">Yes, computers can perform tasks once reserved for humans (arithmetics, schedules, proofs).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1059" data-end="1153">
<p data-start="1061" data-end="1153">But <strong data-start="1065" data-end="1151">“no computer can think in the creative and higher associative senses of the word.”</strong></p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="1155" data-end="1380">That distinction — between combinatorial search or recombination, and genuine creative insight — echoes today’s debates about whether AI can <em data-start="1296" data-end="1304">really</em> be creative or whether it just stitches together parts of prior material.</p>
<hr data-start="1382" data-end="1385">
<h3 data-start="1387" data-end="1421">3. Early “Learning Machines”</h3>
<p data-start="1422" data-end="1557">The footnote about <strong data-start="1441" data-end="1498">“learning,” “nondeterministic,” and “bionic” machines</strong> is telling. Already by the mid-1950s, there was a split:</p>
<ul data-start="1558" data-end="1803">
<li data-start="1558" data-end="1658">
<p data-start="1560" data-end="1658"><strong data-start="1560" data-end="1597">Deterministic automatic computers</strong>: what the book is about — sequential, rule-bound machines.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1659" data-end="1803">
<p data-start="1661" data-end="1803"><strong data-start="1661" data-end="1682">Learning machines</strong>: experimental designs inspired by biology and nondeterminism (what we might now call adaptive systems or AI research).</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="1805" data-end="1986">They even take care to distinguish a <strong data-start="1842" data-end="1862">learning machine</strong> from a <strong data-start="1870" data-end="1890">teaching machine</strong> (a Skinner-style programmed instruction device). This shows how unsettled the vocabulary was.</p>
<hr data-start="1988" data-end="1991">
<h3 data-start="1993" data-end="2023">4. Historical Continuity</h3>
<p data-start="2024" data-end="2088">It’s remarkable how this maps directly onto today’s landscape:</p>
<ul data-start="2089" data-end="2313">
<li data-start="2089" data-end="2167">
<p data-start="2091" data-end="2167">Then: “Automatic computers can play chess, prove theorems, compose music.”</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2168" data-end="2223">
<p data-start="2170" data-end="2223">Now: “AI can play Go, write code, generate images.”</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2224" data-end="2266">
<p data-start="2226" data-end="2266">Then: “But they don’t <em data-start="2248" data-end="2256">really</em> think.”</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2267" data-end="2313">
<p data-start="2269" data-end="2313">Now: “But they don’t <em data-start="2290" data-end="2298">really</em> understand.”</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="2315" data-end="2421">The scale and context have shifted, but the <strong data-start="2359" data-end="2418">philosophical pattern of the debate is nearly unchanged</strong>.</p>
<hr data-start="2423" data-end="2426">
<p data-start="2428" data-end="2663" data-is-last-node="" data-is-only-node="">Would you like me to expand this into a <strong data-start="2468" data-end="2494">comparative commentary</strong> — lining up that 1955 definition of an automatic computer against how we define “AI systems” today, point-by-point? That would make the historical parallels very sharp.</p></div></div></div></div><div class="flex min-h-[46px] justify-start"><div class="touch:-me-2 touch:-ms-3.5 -ms-2.5 -me-1 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-y-4 p-1 select-none touch:w-[calc(100%+--spacing(3.5))] -mt-1 w-[calc(100%+--spacing(2.5))] duration-[1.5s] focus-within:transition-none hover:transition-none pointer-events-none [mask-image:linear-gradient(to_right,black_33%,transparent_66%)] [mask-size:300%_100%] [mask-position:100%_0%] motion-safe:transition-[mask-position] group-hover/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-hover/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] group-focus-within/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-focus-within/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] has-data-[state=open]:pointer-events-auto has-data-[state=open]:[mask-position:0_0]" style="mask-position: 0% 0%;"><button class="text-token-text-secondary hover:bg-token-bg-secondary rounded-lg" aria-label="Copy" aria-pressed="false" data-testid="copy-turn-action-button" data-state="closed"><span class="flex items-center justify-center touch:w-10 h-8 w-8"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="currentColor" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="icon"><path d="M12.668 10.667C12.668 9.95614 12.668 9.46258 12.6367 9.0791C12.6137 8.79732 12.5758 8.60761 12.5244 8.46387L12.4688 8.33399C12.3148 8.03193 12.0803 7.77885 11.793 7.60254L11.666 7.53125C11.508 7.45087 11.2963 7.39395 10.9209 7.36328C10.5374 7.33197 10.0439 7.33203 9.33301 7.33203H6.5C5.78896 7.33203 5.29563 7.33195 4.91211 7.36328C4.63016 7.38632 4.44065 7.42413 4.29688 7.47559L4.16699 7.53125C3.86488 7.68518 3.61186 7.9196 3.43555 8.20703L3.36524 8.33399C3.28478 8.4