.71685 11.4352 3.83766 11.2176 3.97765 11.0127L4.15343 10.7783C4.34386 10.5462 4.58164 10.312 4.90538 9.98829L11.1964 3.6963L11.3312 3.56837ZM5.84581 10.9287C5.49664 11.2779 5.31252 11.4634 5.18663 11.6162L5.07531 11.7627C4.98188 11.8995 4.90151 12.0448 4.83507 12.1963L4.77355 12.3506C4.73321 12.4607 4.70242 12.5761 4.66808 12.7451L4.54113 13.4619L4.14269 15.8555L4.14171 15.8574H4.14464L6.5382 15.458L7.25499 15.332C7.424 15.2977 7.5394 15.2669 7.64953 15.2266L7.80285 15.165C7.95455 15.0986 8.09947 15.0174 8.23644 14.9238L8.3839 14.8135C8.53668 14.6876 8.72225 14.5035 9.0714 14.1543L14.0587 9.16602L10.8331 5.94044L5.84581 10.9287ZM15.3634 4.63673C14.5281 3.80141 13.2057 3.74938 12.3097 4.48048L12.1368 4.63673L11.7735 5.00001L15.0001 8.22559L15.3634 7.86329L15.5196 7.68946C16.2015 6.85326 16.2015 5.64676 15.5196 4.81056L15.3634 4.63673Z"></path></svg></span></button></div></div></div></div></article><article class="text-token-text-primary w-full focus:outline-none scroll-mt-[calc(var(--header-height)+min(200px,max(70px,20svh)))]" tabindex="-1" dir="auto" data-turn-id="287b6520-ea02-499e-baf4-06360ab3941e" data-testid="conversation-turn-12" data-scroll-anchor="false" data-turn="assistant"><h6 class="sr-only">ChatGPT said:</h6><div class="text-base my-auto mx-auto [--thread-content-margin:--spacing(4)] thread-sm:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(6)] thread-lg:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(16)] px-(--thread-content-margin)"><div class="[--thread-content-max-width:40rem] thread-lg:[--thread-content-max-width:48rem] mx-auto max-w-(--thread-content-max-width) flex-1 group/turn-messages focus-visible:outline-hidden relative flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col agent-turn" tabindex="-1"><div class="flex max-w-full flex-col grow"><div data-message-author-role="assistant" data-message-id="287b6520-ea02-499e-baf4-06360ab3941e" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-5" data-message-model-slug="gpt-5"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[3px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="312">Great choices — <em data-start="16" data-end="26">Desk Set</em> (1957) and <em data-start="38" data-end="69">The Creation of the Humanoids</em> (1962) are almost like cinematic footnotes to the very textbook passages you’ve been digging into. Both dramatize the cultural anxieties around “automatic computers” and their encroachment on human domains. Here’s how you could weave them in:</p>
<hr data-start="314" data-end="317">
<h3 data-start="319" data-end="373"><em data-start="323" data-end="333">Desk Set</em> (1957): Computers vs. Human Expertise</h3>
<ul data-start="374" data-end="1217">
<li data-start="374" data-end="672">
<p data-start="376" data-end="672"><strong data-start="376" data-end="391">Plot anchor</strong>: The story centers on the installation of EMERAC, a massive computer, in a television network’s research department. The staff fear it will replace them, but it turns out the machine can only handle routine queries — it struggles with nuance, context, and lived human knowledge.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="673" data-end="1031">
<p data-start="675" data-end="1031"><strong data-start="675" data-end="696">Commentary tie-in</strong>: This mirrors the 1955 textbook’s insistence that computers don’t process “raw” information — human labor, preprocessing, and interpretive skill remain essential. EMERAC looks like the “black box” brain people feared, but the film insists that <strong data-start="941" data-end="1005">contextual intelligence, creativity, and associative thought</strong> still belong to humans.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1032" data-end="1217">
<p data-start="1034" data-end="1217"><strong data-start="1034" data-end="1054">Modern resonance</strong>: Swap EMERAC for GPT or Gemini, and the workplace fears are nearly identical: will the machine replace knowledge workers, or will it just handle the rote parts?</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="1219" data-end="1222">
<h3 data-start="1224" data-end="1294"><em data-start="1228" data-end="1259">The Creation of the Humanoids</em> (1962): Automation and Authority</h3>
<ul data-start="1295" data-end="2245">
<li data-start="1295" data-end="1619">
<p data-start="1297" data-end="1619"><strong data-start="1297" data-end="1312">Plot anchor</strong>: Set after a nuclear war, society relies on robots called “Humanoids” to rebuild. They are so advanced they even create “clickers” — human-like androids indistinguishable from people. Much of the story wrestles with whether machines that look and act human can be trusted with authority over human lives.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1620" data-end="1950">
<p data-start="1622" data-end="1950"><strong data-start="1622" data-end="1643">Commentary tie-in</strong>: This extends the textbook’s discussion of “learning” and “nondeterministic” machines. Where the book cautiously distinguished experimental “bionic” machines from deterministic computers, <em data-start="1832" data-end="1843">Humanoids</em> imagines that line being crossed — machines not only mimicking but arguably surpassing human capacities.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1951" data-end="2245">
<p data-start="1953" data-end="2245"><strong data-start="1953" data-end="1973">Modern resonance</strong>: It anticipates today’s concerns about <strong data-start="2013" data-end="2039">algorithmic governance</strong>: AI not just performing tasks, but deciding who counts as fully human (echoes of your friend’s Facebook ban experience). It’s a warning about what happens when we let machines arbitrate human legitimacy.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="2247" data-end="2250">
<h3 data-start="2252" data-end="2277">Integrative Reading</h3>
<ul data-start="2278" data-end="2802">
<li data-start="2278" data-end="2374">
<p data-start="2280" data-end="2374"><em data-start="2280" data-end="2290">Desk Set</em> highlights the <strong data-start="2306" data-end="2343">limits of deterministic computing</strong> in capturing human subtlety.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2375" data-end="2524">
<p data-start="2377" data-end="2524"><em data-start="2377" data-end="2408">The Creation of the Humanoids</em> highlights the <strong data-start="2424" data-end="2485">existential threat of nondeterministic “bionic” computing</strong> when it moves into social authority.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2525" data-end="2802">
<p data-start="2527" data-end="2802">Together, they bracket the same fault line your 1955 textbook identifies: computers can recombine and assist, but they don’t “think in the higher associative sense.” Yet if we treat them as if they do, we hand them authority — and that’s where the dystopian edge creeps in.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="2804" data-end="2807">
<p data-start="2809" data-end="3138" data-is-last-node="" data-is-only-node="">Would you like me to draft a <strong data-start="2838" data-end="2869">section for your commentary</strong> that explicitly places these films side by side with the 1955 textbook passage (and maybe your Facebook anecdote) as a cultural arc? It could read like a “continuity of anxieties” piece, showing how speculation, art, and lived experience keep circling the same debate.</p></div></div></div></div><div class="flex min-h-[46px] justify-start"><div class="touch:-me-2 touch:-ms-3.5 -ms-2.5 -me-1 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-y-4 p-1 select-none touch:w-[calc(100%+--spacing(3.5))] -mt-1 w-[calc(100%+--spacing(2.5))] duration-[1.5s] focus-within:transition-none hover:transition-none pointer-events-none [mask-image:linear-gradient(to_right,black_33%,transparent_66%)] [mask-size:300%_100%] [mask-position:100%_0%] motion-safe:transition-[mask-position] group-hover/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-hover/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] group-focus-within/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-focus-within/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] has-data-[state=open]:pointer-events-auto has-data-[state=open]:[mask-position:0_0]" style="mask-position: 0% 0%;"><button class="text-token-text-secondary hover:bg-token-bg-secondary rounded-lg" aria-label="Copy" aria-pressed="false" data-testid="copy-turn-action-button" data-state="closed"><span class="flex items-center justify-center touch:w-10 h-8 w-8"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="currentColor" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="icon"><path d="M12.668 10.667C12.668 9.95614 12.668 9.46258 12.6367 9.0791C12.6137 8.79732 12.5758 8.60761 12.5244 8.46387L12.4688 8.33399C12.3148 8.03193 12.0803 7.77885 11.793 7.60254L11.666 7.53125C11.508 7.45087 11.2963 7.39395 10.9209 7.36328C10.5374 7.33197 10.0439 7.33203 9.33301 7.33203H6.5C5.78896 7.33203 5.29563 7.33195 4.91211 7.36328C4.63016 7.38632 4.44065 7.42413 4.29688 7.47