779 5.31252 11.4634 5.18663 11.6162L5.07531 11.7627C4.98188 11.8995 4.90151 12.0448 4.83507 12.1963L4.77355 12.3506C4.73321 12.4607 4.70242 12.5761 4.66808 12.7451L4.54113 13.4619L4.14269 15.8555L4.14171 15.8574H4.14464L6.5382 15.458L7.25499 15.332C7.424 15.2977 7.5394 15.2669 7.64953 15.2266L7.80285 15.165C7.95455 15.0986 8.09947 15.0174 8.23644 14.9238L8.3839 14.8135C8.53668 14.6876 8.72225 14.5035 9.0714 14.1543L14.0587 9.16602L10.8331 5.94044L5.84581 10.9287ZM15.3634 4.63673C14.5281 3.80141 13.2057 3.74938 12.3097 4.48048L12.1368 4.63673L11.7735 5.00001L15.0001 8.22559L15.3634 7.86329L15.5196 7.68946C16.2015 6.85326 16.2015 5.64676 15.5196 4.81056L15.3634 4.63673Z"></path></svg></span></button></div></div></div></div></article><article class="text-token-text-primary w-full focus:outline-none scroll-mt-[calc(var(--header-height)+min(200px,max(70px,20svh)))]" tabindex="-1" dir="auto" data-turn-id="ddbc563b-3fd9-4e52-b1bd-58eca9b24da3" data-testid="conversation-turn-14" data-scroll-anchor="false" data-turn="assistant"><h6 class="sr-only">ChatGPT said:</h6><div class="text-base my-auto mx-auto [--thread-content-margin:--spacing(4)] thread-sm:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(6)] thread-lg:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(16)] px-(--thread-content-margin)"><div class="[--thread-content-max-width:40rem] thread-lg:[--thread-content-max-width:48rem] mx-auto max-w-(--thread-content-max-width) flex-1 group/turn-messages focus-visible:outline-hidden relative flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col agent-turn" tabindex="-1"><div class="flex max-w-full flex-col grow"><div data-message-author-role="assistant" data-message-id="ddbc563b-3fd9-4e52-b1bd-58eca9b24da3" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-5" data-message-model-slug="gpt-5"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[3px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="213">Here’s a draft you could drop into your commentary as a <strong data-start="56" data-end="89">cultural through-line section</strong>, tying the 1955 textbook to <em data-start="118" data-end="128">Desk Set</em> (1957), <em data-start="137" data-end="168">The Creation of the Humanoids</em> (1962), and the present Facebook anecdote:</p>
<hr data-start="215" data-end="218">
<h2 data-start="220" data-end="269">Automatic Computers and Cultural Imagination</h2>
<p data-start="271" data-end="681">The 1955 <em data-start="280" data-end="317">Introduction to Automatic Computers</em> sought to demystify the “black box” fantasy of machines that could casually generate knowledge. It stressed that data had to be collected, verified, and pre-processed by skilled human labor, and that computers could not “think in the creative and higher associative senses.” This sober reminder against misplaced anthropomorphism echoed through popular culture.</p>
<h3 data-start="683" data-end="732"><em data-start="687" data-end="697">Desk Set</em> (1957): Anxiety in the Workplace</h3>
<p data-start="733" data-end="1278">In <em data-start="736" data-end="746">Desk Set</em>, the television network’s research department faces the arrival of EMERAC, a room-sized computer designed to handle reference queries. The staff fear replacement, echoing the textbook’s insistence that machines only manipulate what humans structure for them. EMERAC is portrayed as powerful yet comically limited: it can retrieve information but not interpret cultural nuance or contextualize language. The film captures a transitional anxiety—computers as both indispensable assistants and opaque intruders into human expertise.</p>
<h3 data-start="1280" data-end="1350"><em data-start="1284" data-end="1315">The Creation of the Humanoids</em> (1962): Authority and Legitimacy</h3>
<p data-start="1351" data-end="1916">By the early 1960s, anxieties had shifted from clerical replacement to existential legitimacy. <em data-start="1446" data-end="1477">The Creation of the Humanoids</em> imagines a society rebuilt by robot “Humanoids” who advance to the point of manufacturing android “clickers” indistinguishable from humans. The question is no longer whether computers can calculate, but whether they can define humanity itself. This extends the textbook’s passing mention of “learning” and “bionic” machines into a speculative dystopia: what happens when nondeterministic designs claim not only competence but authority?</p>
<h3 data-start="1918" data-end="1970">From Early Computing to Algorithmic Governance</h3>
<p data-start="1971" data-end="2399">Fast-forward to the present: a friend receives a permanent Facebook ban, with no appeal, imposed automatically by an AI moderation system. The dystopian element lies not in the sophistication of the AI—it is neither creative nor associative—but in its <strong data-start="2223" data-end="2249">unquestioned authority</strong>. The opacity of the decision, combined with the centrality of digital platforms to everyday life, makes the ban feel like a <em data-start="2374" data-end="2388">Black Mirror</em> episode.</p>
<h3 data-start="2401" data-end="2430">Continuity of Anxieties</h3>
<ul data-start="2431" data-end="2731">
<li data-start="2431" data-end="2500">
<p data-start="2433" data-end="2500">1955: Computers are not brains, they require pre-processed input.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2501" data-end="2580">
<p data-start="2503" data-end="2580">1957: EMERAC is a tool, not a replacement, but it destabilizes human roles.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2581" data-end="2657">
<p data-start="2583" data-end="2657">1962: Humanoids blur the line between mechanical aid and human identity.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2658" data-end="2731">
<p data-start="2660" data-end="2731">2025: AI moderates and governs without recourse, eroding due process.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="2733" data-end="3210">Across seventy years, the dialogue has hardly shifted: Are machines tools, partners, or judges? What remains constant is the temptation to project human categories—“thinking,” “learning,” “judging”—onto systems that in practice remain deterministic, brittle, and dependent on human scaffolding. What has changed is not the argument, but the <strong data-start="3074" data-end="3098">stakes of dependence</strong>: from scarce machines in laboratories to infrastructural systems upon which everyone’s daily life now relies.</p>
<hr data-start="3212" data-end="3215">
<p data-start="3217" data-end="3425" data-is-last-node="" data-is-only-node="">Do you want me to keep this as a <strong data-start="3250" data-end="3278">standalone essay section</strong> (like above), or integrate it into a broader <strong data-start="3324" data-end="3354">historical timeline format</strong> that runs from 1955 texts through film and into present AI governance?</p></div></div></div></div><div class="flex min-h-[46px] justify-start"><div class="touch:-me-2 touch:-ms-3.5 -ms-2.5 -me-1 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-y-4 p-1 select-none touch:w-[calc(100%+--spacing(3.5))] -mt-1 w-[calc(100%+--spacing(2.5))] duration-[1.5s] focus-within:transition-none hover:transition-none pointer-events-none [mask-image:linear-gradient(to_right,black_33%,transparent_66%)] [mask-size:300%_100%] [mask-position:100%_0%] motion-safe:transition-[mask-position] group-hover/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-hover/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] group-focus-within/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-focus-within/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] has-data-[state=open]:pointer-events-auto has-data-[state=open]:[mask-position:0_0]" style="mask-position: 0% 0%;"><button class="text-token-text-secondary hover:bg-token-bg-secondary rounded-lg" aria-label="Copy" aria-pressed="false" data-testid="copy-turn-action-button" data-state="closed"><span class="flex items-center justify-center touch:w-10 h-8 w-8"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="currentColor" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="icon"><path d="M12.668 10.667C12.668 9.95614 12.668 9.46258 12.6367 9.0791C12.6137 8.79732 12.5758 8.60761 12.5244 8.46387L12.4688 8.33399C12.3148 8.03193 12.0803 7.77885 11.793 7.60254L11.666 7.53125C11.508 7.45087 11.2963 7.39395 10.9209 7.36328C10.5374 7.33197 10.0439 7.33203 9.33301 7.33203H6.5C5.78896 7.33203 5.29563 7.33195 4.91211 7.36328C4.63016 7.38632 4.44065 7.42413 4.29688 7.47559L4.16699 7.53125C3.86488 7.68518 3.61186 7.9196 3.43555 8.20703L3.36524