 flex-1 group/turn-messages focus-visible:outline-hidden relative flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col agent-turn" tabindex="-1"><div class="flex max-w-full flex-col grow"><div data-message-author-role="assistant" data-message-id="d58e12b4-6a42-4209-92e3-b5421727dc10" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-5" data-message-model-slug="gpt-5"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[3px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="409">That piece by Jerry Neumann is a good example of the same historical pattern we’ve been circling with <em data-start="102" data-end="123">automatic computers</em>, <em data-start="125" data-end="135">Desk Set</em>, and <em data-start="141" data-end="172">The Creation of the Humanoids</em>. It frames generative AI not as a once-in-a-century gold rush (like the microprocessor or electricity) but as a <em data-start="285" data-end="319">shipping containerization moment</em> — transformative for society, but not especially profitable for founders and investors.</p>
<p data-start="411" data-end="449">Here’s how it links into our thread:</p>
<hr data-start="451" data-end="454">
<h3 data-start="456" data-end="494">1. Cycles of Promise and Capture</h3>
<ul data-start="495" data-end="1101">
<li data-start="495" data-end="739">
<p data-start="497" data-end="739"><strong data-start="497" data-end="524">Microprocessor (1970s):</strong> Permissionless tinkering, cheap chips (6502, Z80), grassroots communities → eventually PCs, spreadsheets, smartphones. Fortunes made because value was distributed into startups, not captured solely by incumbents.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="740" data-end="906">
<p data-start="742" data-end="906"><strong data-start="742" data-end="774">Shipping Containers (1950s):</strong> Revolutionized global trade, but value diffused so broadly across the supply chain that investors saw little concentrated return.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="907" data-end="1101">
<p data-start="909" data-end="1101"><strong data-start="909" data-end="935">Generative AI (2020s):</strong> Neumann argues it’s more like containers than chips: powerful, yes, but the value gets competed away into thin margins, captured by customers or a few oligopolies.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="1103" data-end="1106">
<h3 data-start="1108" data-end="1140">2. Continuity of Anxieties</h3>
<ul data-start="1141" data-end="1540">
<li data-start="1141" data-end="1219">
<p data-start="1143" data-end="1219"><strong data-start="1143" data-end="1162">1955 textbooks:</strong> “Computers can’t <em data-start="1180" data-end="1188">really</em> think, they only recombine.”</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1220" data-end="1312">
<p data-start="1222" data-end="1312"><strong data-start="1222" data-end="1242">1957 <em data-start="1229" data-end="1239">Desk Set</em>:</strong> EMERAC as threat to human jobs, but really just a clumsy assistant.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1313" data-end="1420">
<p data-start="1315" data-end="1420"><strong data-start="1315" data-end="1356">1962 <em data-start="1322" data-end="1353">The Creation of the Humanoids</em>:</strong> What if machines claim <em data-start="1380" data-end="1391">authority</em> over what counts as human?</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1421" data-end="1540">
<p data-start="1423" data-end="1540"><strong data-start="1423" data-end="1440">2025 Neumann:</strong> What if AI doesn’t even make us rich? The anxiety shifts from <em data-start="1503" data-end="1519">can they think</em> to <em data-start="1523" data-end="1537">can they pay</em>.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="1542" data-end="1662">Each era finds a different edge of discomfort: epistemic (thinking), existential (humanity), economic (profitability).</p>
<hr data-start="1664" data-end="1667">
<h3 data-start="1669" data-end="1694">3. RSVP Perspective</h3>
<p data-start="1695" data-end="2112">From an RSVP lens, Neumann is pointing out that <strong data-start="1743" data-end="1864">AI as an entropic infrastructure redistributes order into the commons rather than concentrating it as private capital</strong>. Like gossip or religion, it stabilizes flows of meaning and efficiency, but it resists enclosure because any advantage leaks outward quickly. Attempts to capture it (paywalls, oligopolies) are metabolized back into the broader system over time.</p>
<hr data-start="2114" data-end="2117">
<h3 data-start="2119" data-end="2162">4. Contemporary Echoes of “Pure Evil”</h3>
<p data-start="2163" data-end="2205">Tie this back to your Facebook anecdote:</p>
<ul data-start="2206" data-end="2637">
<li data-start="2206" data-end="2307">
<p data-start="2208" data-end="2307">Platforms deploy AI to govern users, but <strong data-start="2249" data-end="2304">the real wealth is not in invention—it’s in control</strong>.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2308" data-end="2461">
<p data-start="2310" data-end="2461">Users experience the “Black Mirror” edge not just from bans, but from the realization that the infrastructure exists for enclosure, not emancipation.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2462" data-end="2637">
<p data-start="2464" data-end="2637">Neumann’s argument shows that even for investors, this control doesn’t necessarily produce new fortunes. It’s a <strong data-start="2576" data-end="2604">zero-sum extraction game</strong> more than a creative frontier.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="2639" data-end="2642">
<p data-start="2644" data-end="2949" data-is-last-node="" data-is-only-node="">Would you like me to fold Neumann’s article into the <strong data-start="2697" data-end="2717">four-stage chart</strong> (gossip → religion → platforms → semantic infrastructures) as the <em data-start="2784" data-end="2794">economic</em> axis, showing how each stage also had its own “profit paradox”? That could sharpen the continuity between cultural anxieties and economic disappointments.</p></div></div></div></div><div class="flex min-h-[46px] justify-start"><div class="touch:-me-2 touch:-ms-3.5 -ms-2.5 -me-1 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-y-4 p-1 select-none touch:w-[calc(100%+--spacing(3.5))] -mt-1 w-[calc(100%+--spacing(2.5))] duration-[1.5s] focus-within:transition-none hover:transition-none pointer-events-none [mask-image:linear-gradient(to_right,black_33%,transparent_66%)] [mask-size:300%_100%] [mask-position:100%_0%] motion-safe:transition-[mask-position] group-hover/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-hover/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] group-focus-within/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-focus-within/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] has-data-[state=open]:pointer-events-auto has-data-[state=open]:[mask-position:0_0]" style="mask-position: 0% 0%;"><button class="text-token-text-secondary hover:bg-token-bg-secondary rounded-lg" aria-label="Copy" aria-pressed="false" data-testid="copy-turn-action-button" data-state="closed"><span class="flex items-center justify-center touch:w-10 h-8 w-8"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="currentColor" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="icon"><path d="M12.668 10.667C12.668 9.95614 12.668 9.46258 12.6367 9.0791C12.6137 8.79732 12.5758 8.60761 12.5244 8.46387L12.4688 8.33399C12.3148 8.03193 12.0803 7.77885 11.793 7.60254L11.666 7.53125C11.508 7.45087 11.2963 7.39395 10.9209 7.36328C10.5374 7.33197 10.0439 7.33203 9.33301 7.33203H6.5C5.78896 7.33203 5.29563 7.33195 4.91211 7.36328C4.63016 7.38632 4.44065 7.4