 dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="104">Got it — thanks for clarifying. That means your textbook went through <strong data-start="70" data-end="101">multiple printings/editions</strong>:</p>
<ul data-start="105" data-end="226">
<li data-start="105" data-end="133">
<p data-start="107" data-end="133"><strong data-start="107" data-end="115">1955</strong>: first edition.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="134" data-end="184">
<p data-start="136" data-end="184"><strong data-start="136" data-end="144">1957</strong>: likely a reprint or revised edition.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="185" data-end="226">
<p data-start="187" data-end="226"><strong data-start="187" data-end="195">1963</strong>: a second edition, expanded.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="228" data-end="369">In citations, you’ll usually want to specify the exact edition you consulted. Here’s how you can represent it in standard form and in BibTeX:</p>
<hr data-start="371" data-end="374">
<h3 data-start="376" data-end="399">Standard references</h3>
<ul data-start="400" data-end="629">
<li data-start="400" data-end="510">
<p data-start="402" data-end="510">Chapin, Ned. <em data-start="415" data-end="456">An Introduction to Automatic Computers.</em> Princeton, NJ: D. Van Nostrand Company, Inc., 1955.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="511" data-end="629">
<p data-start="513" data-end="629">Chapin, Ned. <em data-start="526" data-end="567">An Introduction to Automatic Computers.</em> 2nd ed. Princeton, NJ: D. Van Nostrand Company, Inc., 1963.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="631" data-end="759">(If your 1957 copy says just “Second Printing” rather than a full new edition, you’d note it as a printing rather than edition.)</p>
