reak-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="79">Here’s a draft abstract that matches the outline and references we assembled:</p>
<hr data-start="81" data-end="84">
<h3 data-start="86" data-end="100">Abstract</h3>
<p data-start="102" data-end="840">This essay examines the continuity of cultural and economic anxieties surrounding computing technologies, from the first textbooks on “automatic computers” in the 1950s to contemporary debates over chokepoint capitalism. Early accounts, such as Ned Chapin’s <em data-start="360" data-end="400">An Introduction to Automatic Computers</em> (1963), warned against treating machines as “brains,” stressing that outputs depended on extensive human preprocessing. Films like <em data-start="532" data-end="542">Desk Set</em> (1957) and <em data-start="554" data-end="585">The Creation of the Humanoids</em> (1962) dramatized these anxieties, staging the tension between computers as assistants, threats, and judges. Today, opaque platform governance—illustrated by algorithmic account bans—extends this lineage, giving machines not intelligence but authority.</p>
<p data-start="842" data-end="1285">Economically, the trajectory also rhymes: whereas the microprocessor enabled permissionless invention and new fortunes, containerization spread value downstream to consumers and incumbents. As Jerry Neumann (2025) argues, generative infrastructures resemble containerization more than the microprocessor: revolutionary in effect, but consolidating wealth into oligopolistic chokepoints rather than spawning broad entrepreneurial opportunity.</p>
<p data-start="1287" data-end="1897">To frame this cycle, the essay proposes a typology of semantic infrastructures—gossip, religion, platforms, and chokepoint capitalism—each emerging as an entropy-smoothing tool that later hardens into constraint. Drawing on the Relativistic Scalar Vector Plenum (RSVP) theory, the essay sketches design principles for a potential “fourth stage”: semantic infrastructures that metabolize co-optation rather than ossifying into rent-seeking chokepoints. The goal is not to deny capture but to circulate it back into the commons, allowing infrastructures to stabilize meaning without collapsing into constraint.</p>
