-end="31">Introduction</strong> that follows from the abstract and sets up the essay’s trajectory:</p>
<hr data-start="102" data-end="105">
<h3 data-start="107" data-end="125">Introduction</h3>
<p data-start="127" data-end="924">Since the mid-twentieth century, computing has been accompanied by a persistent set of cultural and economic anxieties. In the 1950s, textbooks such as Ned Chapin’s <em data-start="292" data-end="332">An Introduction to Automatic Computers</em> (1963) warned readers against imagining machines as autonomous “giant brains,” emphasizing instead the skilled human labor that went into preparing data and programming instructions. Yet almost immediately, public discourse and popular media framed computers in precisely those anthropomorphic terms. Films like <em data-start="645" data-end="655">Desk Set</em> (1957) depicted giant room-sized machines as potential replacements for human experts, while <em data-start="749" data-end="780">The Creation of the Humanoids</em> (1962) envisioned a society in which machines not only performed human tasks but also claimed authority over the very definition of humanity.</p>
<p data-start="926" data-end="1746">The continuity of this discourse is striking. Early commentators argued that computers could not “think in the higher associative sense,” but they might still play chess, prove theorems, or compose music. Today, the same argument is repeated almost verbatim about generative systems: they can simulate conversation, draft essays, or generate images, but critics ask whether they <em data-start="1305" data-end="1313">really</em> understand. The vocabulary has changed, but the structure of the debate—machines as tools, partners, or judges—remains the same. What has changed is scale. In 1955 there were a handful of institutional machines; in 2025, automated systems govern billions of users, often invisibly. A Facebook ban imposed by algorithm with no appeal is not just a matter of technical curiosity but a matter of livelihood, reputation, and identity.</p>
<p data-start="1748" data-end="2365">Alongside the cultural narrative, the economics of computing have followed a parallel trajectory. Some technologies, like the microprocessor, created fortunes by enabling distributed experimentation and new entrepreneurial ecosystems. Others, like container shipping, transformed society but concentrated profits downstream, reinforcing incumbents rather than generating new fortunes. As Jerry Neumann (2025) argues, generative infrastructures belong to the latter category: revolutionary in their effects, but arriving at a moment of consolidation that rewards oligopolies and consumers rather than new innovators.</p>
<p data-start="2367" data-end="3057">This essay traces these continuities across four stages of semantic infrastructure: gossip, religion, platforms, and what can be called chokepoint capitalism. Each stage emerges as an entropy-smoothing mechanism that stabilizes cooperation and meaning, only to harden into constraint. To interpret this cycle, the essay employs the Relativistic Scalar Vector Plenum (RSVP) framework, which models semantic systems in terms of scalar density (Φ), vector flows (𝒗), and entropy (S). From this perspective, the goal is not to deny co-optation but to design infrastructures that metabolize it, recirculating value into the commons rather than ossifying into dogma, bureaucracy, or oligopoly.</p>
