ds dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="130">Here’s a draft <strong data-start="15" data-end="35">bridging section</strong> that you can slot between the Introduction and the historical discussion of 1950s computing:</p>
<hr data-start="132" data-end="135">
<h3 data-start="137" data-end="180">The Cycle of Semantic Infrastructures</h3>
<p data-start="182" data-end="540">To situate the history of computing within a longer arc, it is useful to think of communication and coordination systems as recurring stages of <strong data-start="326" data-end="353">semantic infrastructure</strong>. Each stage begins as a negentropic innovation that stabilizes cooperation, but as it matures, it hardens into a structure of constraint. Four such stages are particularly instructive.</p>
<p data-start="542" data-end="789"><strong data-start="542" data-end="552">Gossip</strong> was the earliest form: a distributed memory system that compressed complex behavior into shareable narratives. It enabled trust and cooperation in small groups but quickly ossified into reputation traps from which there was no appeal.</p>
<p data-start="791" data-end="1066"><strong data-start="791" data-end="803">Religion</strong> extended this logic to scale. By codifying cosmologies, rituals, and moral codes, it aligned large populations across generations. Yet its very durability turned into hierarchy and dogma, with institutions exercising coercive authority over thought and action.</p>
<p data-start="1068" data-end="1429"><strong data-start="1068" data-end="1081">Platforms</strong> are the digital heirs of gossip and religion. Promising connection and frictionless communication, they achieved planetary scale but at the cost of enshittification, opaque governance, surveillance, and the universal assumption of the consumer with surplus income. Where gossip was reputational and religion doctrinal, platforms are algorithmic.</p>
<p data-start="1431" data-end="1803">Finally, <strong data-start="1440" data-end="1465">chokepoint capitalism</strong> marks the current stage. As Jerry Neumann (2025) has argued by analogy to containerization, today’s generative infrastructures are revolutionary in effect but consolidating in economics. Value is created, but capture occurs at oligopolistic chokepoints, leaving innovators and workers squeezed while consumers reap most of the benefit.</p>
<p data-start="1805" data-end="2161">Seen in sequence, these stages illustrate a persistent cycle: emergent systems arise to smooth entropy and generate order, only to be captured, ossified, and weaponized as constraints. This cycle provides the backdrop for the more specific cultural and economic narratives of computing, from the textbooks of the 1950s to the platform dystopias of today.</p>
