start="0" data-end="136">Here’s a draft <strong data-start="15" data-end="35">Historical Frame</strong> section that follows the bridging discussion and anchors the essay in the 1950s computing context:</p>
<hr data-start="138" data-end="141">
<h3 data-start="143" data-end="203">The Historical Frame: Automatic Computers in the 1950s</h3>
<p data-start="205" data-end="947">By the mid-1950s, the term <em data-start="232" data-end="252">automatic computer</em> had entered both technical and popular vocabularies, carrying with it a potent mix of awe and misunderstanding. Ned Chapin’s <em data-start="378" data-end="418">An Introduction to Automatic Computers</em> (2nd ed., 1963) exemplifies the effort to demystify the new technology. Chapin cautioned against the popular image of the computer as a “black box” into which raw data could be poured and out of which finished answers would flow. In reality, data had to be collected, verified, coded, and prepared at great human cost before a machine could produce even a single “small report.” Far from being autonomous intelligences, computers were framed as elaborate but rigidly deterministic tools, dependent on meticulous preprocessing.</p>
<p data-start="949" data-end="1654">This sober framing was set against a broader debate already in motion. Edmund C. Berkeley’s <em data-start="1041" data-end="1079">Giant Brains, or Machines That Think</em> (1949) argued that computers should be understood as machines that could, in a limited sense, “think.” Alan Turing’s famous essay “Computing Machinery and Intelligence” (1950), reprinted in James Newman’s <em data-start="1285" data-end="1311">The World of Mathematics</em> (1956), framed the question more precisely: not “do machines think?” but “can their performance be indistinguishable from human reasoning?” These early positions already mapped the terrain of argument that still dominates today: whether computational reproduction of human-like tasks counts as intelligence, or merely as mechanical mimicry.</p>
<p data-start="1656" data-end="2396">The professional literature of the period reinforced this tension. Samuel Williams’s <em data-start="1741" data-end="1768">Digital Computing Systems</em> (1959), Roger Nett and Stanley Hetzler’s <em data-start="1810" data-end="1857">An Introduction to Electronic Data Processing</em> (1959), and Robert Gregory and Richard Van Horn’s <em data-start="1908" data-end="1943">Automatic Data Processing Systems</em> (1960) positioned computers primarily as business and administrative tools. They emphasized efficiency, error reduction, and new forms of managerial control rather than speculation about artificial thought. In other words, while philosophical discourse leaned toward the mind–machine question, professional manuals steered students and practitioners toward the prosaic but transformative domains of accounting, scheduling, and information management.</p>
<p data-start="2398" data-end="2976">Taken together, these texts illustrate the cultural duality of early computing: at once a technical apparatus of extraordinary promise and a conceptual object onto which debates about intelligence, creativity, and human uniqueness were projected. What was still scarce in this period was not anxiety but <em data-start="2702" data-end="2709">scale</em>: the number of machines was small, their audiences were specialized, and their impact—though widely heralded—was still largely confined to institutions. It was this scarcity that allowed both utopian projection and sober delimitation to coexist without resolution.</p>
