data-end="41">Cultural Imaginaries</strong> section that follows directly from the historical frame:</p>
<hr data-start="102" data-end="105">
<h3 data-start="107" data-end="181">Cultural Imaginaries: From Clerical Anxiety to Existential Authority</h3>
<p data-start="183" data-end="508">If mid-century textbooks sought to discipline the imagination of computing, popular culture provided the opposite: dramatizations that amplified hopes and fears. Two films from the late 1950s and early 1960s, <em data-start="392" data-end="402">Desk Set</em> (1957) and <em data-start="414" data-end="445">The Creation of the Humanoids</em> (1962), capture distinct moments in this cultural imaginary.</p>
<p data-start="510" data-end="1385"><strong data-start="510" data-end="542">Clerical Anxiety: <em data-start="530" data-end="540">Desk Set</em></strong><br data-start="542" data-end="545">
Walter Lang’s <em data-start="559" data-end="569">Desk Set</em> is ostensibly a romantic comedy but also a workplace fable about the arrival of EMERAC, a massive “electronic brain” installed in a television network’s research department. The staff of reference librarians, threatened by redundancy, embody the anxieties Chapin and others sought to calm: the fear that machines would effortlessly replace human skill. EMERAC, however, is portrayed as a limited assistant, easily confused by context and nuance. The lesson is not that machines will think, but that they will destabilize workplaces by demanding new forms of human oversight. <em data-start="1145" data-end="1155">Desk Set</em> thus dramatizes the very warning found in the textbooks: computers are not autonomous intelligences, but they reshape labor and expertise in ways that feel threatening to those whose livelihoods depend on specialized knowledge.</p>
<p data-start="1387" data-end="2169"><strong data-start="1387" data-end="1445">Existential Authority: <em data-start="1412" data-end="1443">The Creation of the Humanoids</em></strong><br data-start="1445" data-end="1448">
By 1962, the cultural register had shifted. Wesley Barry’s <em data-start="1507" data-end="1538">The Creation of the Humanoids</em> imagines a post-apocalyptic society rebuilt by highly advanced robots. These machines go beyond clerical substitution, raising the specter of replacement at the level of identity itself. The humanoids not only labor alongside humans but also manufacture android “clickers” that are indistinguishable from their creators. The central question is no longer whether machines can compute or retrieve facts, but whether they can claim the prerogative to define humanity. This is a far cry from Chapin’s careful reminder that computers merely execute prepared instructions. Here the machine’s authority is speculative and existential.</p>
<p data-start="2171" data-end="2593"><strong data-start="2171" data-end="2198">From Assistant to Judge</strong><br data-start="2198" data-end="2201">
Together, the films illustrate the widening arc of cultural anxiety. <em data-start="2270" data-end="2280">Desk Set</em> stages the fear of displacement within the bureaucratic workplace; <em data-start="2348" data-end="2379">The Creation of the Humanoids</em> projects the fear of domination within society at large. Both extend the philosophical debates of Berkeley and Turing into narrative form, mapping the transition from “machines as tools” to “machines as judges.”</p>
<p data-start="2595" data-end="3053">This cultural imaginary did not emerge in isolation. It built upon the scarcity and mystique of early computers, exaggerating their capacity while also crystallizing the anxieties of clerks, managers, and citizens whose daily lives were increasingly mediated by information. As with gossip and religion in earlier eras, the narratives simplified complex realities into easily transmissible forms—rumors, doctrines, films—that circulated faster than nuance.</p>
<hr data-start="3055" data-end="3060">
<p data-start="3062" data-end="3353" data-is-last-node="" data-is-only-node="">Do you want me to continue directly into the <strong data-start="3107" data-end="3134">Platforms as Enclosures</strong> section (linking these cultural imaginaries to your Facebook anecdote and the governance-by-algorithm problem), or first insert a short <strong data-start="3271" data-end="3295">transition paragraph</strong> that ties the films back to the 1950s textbook discourse?</p></div></div></div></div><div class="flex min-h-[46px] justify-start"><div class="touch:-me-2 touch:-ms-3.5 -ms-2.5 -me-1 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-y-4 p-1 select-none touch:w-[calc(100%+--spacing(3.5))] -mt-1 w-[calc(100%+--spacing(2.5))] duration-[1.5s] focus-within:transition-none hover:transition-none pointer-events-none [mask-image:linear-gradient(to_right,black_33%,transparent_66%)] [mask-size:300%_100%] [mask-position:100%_0%] motion-safe:transition-[mask-position] group-hover/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-hover/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] group-focus-within/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-focus-within/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] has-data-[state=open]:pointer-events-auto has-data-[state=open]:[mask-position:0_0]" style="mask-position: 0% 0%;"><button class="text-token-text-secondary hover:bg-token-bg-secondary rounded-lg" aria-label="Copy" aria-pressed="false" data-testid="copy-turn-action-button" data-state="closed"><span class="flex items-center justify-center touch:w-10 h-8 w-8"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="currentColor" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="icon"><path d="M12.668 10.667C12.668 9.95614 12.668 9.46258 12.6367 9.0791C12.6137 8.79732 12.5758 8.60761 12.5244 8.46387L12.4688 8.33399C12.3148 8.03193 12.0803 7.77885 11.793 7.60254L11.666 7.53125C11.508 7.45087 11.2963 7.39395 10.9209 7.36328C10.5374 7.33197 10.0439 7.33203 9.33301 7.33203H6.5C5.78896 7.33203 5.29563 7.33195 4.91211 7.36328C4.63016 7.38632 4.44065 7.42413 4.2