 dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="194">Here’s a <strong data-start="9" data-end="33">transition paragraph</strong> that ties the cultural imaginaries (<em data-start="70" data-end="80">Desk Set</em> and <em data-start="85" data-end="116">The Creation of the Humanoids</em>) back to the 1950s textbook discourse, and sets up the move into platforms:</p>
<hr data-start="196" data-end="199">
<h3 data-start="201" data-end="263">Transition: From Textbook Warnings to Platform Realities</h3>
<p data-start="265" data-end="1034">What is striking is how closely these cultural artifacts rhymed with the sober discourse of the textbooks. Chapin (1963) warned readers that automatic computers could not think creatively but might still play chess or compose music; <em data-start="498" data-end="508">Desk Set</em> dramatized this by showing a computer capable of retrieval but incapable of contextual judgment. Where Berkeley speculated about “giant brains” and Turing proposed tests of indistinguishability, <em data-start="704" data-end="735">The Creation of the Humanoids</em> projected the same questions into a speculative future, asking whether machines might ultimately acquire not just intelligence but social authority. Both technical manuals and popular films thus rehearsed the same categories—tool, partner, judge—even as they disagreed about feasibility and risk.</p>
<p data-start="1036" data-end="1691">What has changed since then is not the content of the debate but its scale. In the 1950s and early 1960s, there were only a handful of institutional computers; their scarcity allowed the discourse to remain abstract. Today, platform infrastructures embed algorithmic systems into nearly every aspect of daily life. A permanent ban issued by a content-moderation algorithm with no appeal is not a thought experiment but an ordinary event. Machines that cannot think in any strong sense nevertheless exercise the authority of judgment. This is the shift from <strong data-start="1593" data-end="1615">imagined anxieties</strong> to <strong data-start="1619" data-end="1639">lived enclosures</strong>, and it marks the entrance into the platform era.</p>
