<hr data-start="57" data-end="60">
<h3 data-start="62" data-end="91">Platforms as Enclosures</h3>
<p data-start="93" data-end="606">If gossip compressed reputation and religion codified doctrine, digital platforms represent the third great enclosure of semantic life. Built on the promise of openness and connection, platforms achieved planetary scale by reducing friction in communication, commerce, and cultural exchange. Yet as their infrastructures matured, they hardened into chokepoints: opaque governance systems, advertising-driven attention funnels, and algorithmic reputations. What had been marketed as liberation became constraint.</p>
<p data-start="608" data-end="1273">The everyday experience of this shift is stark. A permanent Facebook ban imposed by an automated moderation system illustrates the new reality. The user is given no reason, no appeal, no interlocutor—only the judgment of an algorithm, treated as binding. The situation is paradoxical. As Ned Chapin reminded readers in 1963, computers cannot “devise a way of solving a new problem” but only follow instructions. Yet on modern platforms, machines that cannot think in any associative or creative sense are endowed with <em data-start="1126" data-end="1137">authority</em>. They are no longer assistants but judges, arbitrating identity, legitimacy, and access to networks of friends, markets, and publics.</p>
<p data-start="1275" data-end="1831">This dynamic produces what critics call <strong data-start="1315" data-end="1340">chokepoint capitalism</strong> (Giblin and Doctorow 2022). Platforms position themselves at key bottlenecks in the flow of communication and value—app stores, ad networks, content feeds, moderation pipelines—and extract rent from their control. The structure mirrors the gossip–religion cycle: tools designed to stabilize meaning end by constraining it. Where gossip froze reputations, and religion ossified belief, platforms lock behavior into monetized channels, demanding users act as both consumers and commodities.</p>
<p data-start="1833" data-end="2245">The opacity of these enclosures intensifies their dystopian character. In <em data-start="1907" data-end="1917">Desk Set</em>, EMERAC’s limitations were comic, its malfunctions immediately visible. On Facebook, the malfunction is systemic: an invisible process that declares a human account invalid without explanation. The “Black Mirror” feeling arises not from machine intelligence but from machine <strong data-start="2193" data-end="2213">unaccountability</strong>—a bureaucracy without appeal.</p>
<p data-start="2247" data-end="2660">This is the condition in which contemporary anxieties about computing are grounded. The textbook debates and cinematic dramatizations have not vanished; they have simply been absorbed into infrastructures that now govern billions. Platforms operationalize the old question—tool, partner, or judge—by silently treating the computer as judge, while obscuring the human hands that programmed the rules of judgment.</p>
