ling"><p data-start="0" data-end="154">Here’s a concise <strong data-start="17" data-end="41">transition paragraph</strong> to bridge from the lived experience of platform bans into the broader economic logic of chokepoint capitalism:</p>
<hr data-start="156" data-end="159">
<h3 data-start="161" data-end="220">Transition: From Lived Constraint to Economic Capture</h3>
<p data-start="222" data-end="925">The experience of automated bans and opaque moderation makes visible, at the individual scale, what is happening structurally across the economy. Platforms do not simply govern communication; they extract value by controlling the passage points through which communication, culture, and commerce must flow. What looks like “algorithmic judgment” to a user is, at the macro level, the same dynamic identified by economic historians as chokepoint capitalism: profits accrue not from invention or creativity, but from occupying bottlenecks where no alternative routes exist. In this sense, the social constraint of the Facebook ban and the economic logic of container shipping belong to the same pattern.</p>
