sition and integrates Neumann’s containerization analogy:</p>
<hr data-start="137" data-end="140">
<h3 data-start="142" data-end="169">Chokepoint Capitalism</h3>
<p data-start="171" data-end="796">If platforms are enclosures, chokepoint capitalism is their economic logic. Rather than producing new fortunes through permissionless experimentation, as the microprocessor did in the 1970s, chokepoint capitalism resembles containerization in the 1950s: revolutionary in effect, but consolidating in outcome. Container shipping transformed global trade, but profits accrued not to the innovators who designed the containers or the companies that operated the ships, but to the downstream incumbents who leveraged efficiency at scale. Walmart and IKEA, not shipbuilders or port authorities, became the primary beneficiaries.</p>
<p data-start="798" data-end="1433">Jerry Neumann (2025) argues that generative infrastructures follow this same pattern. They arrive not at the beginning of a new techno-economic wave, but at the end of the ICT cycle, when surprise is absent and experimentation is constrained. Entrepreneurs cannot build the equivalent of Apple or Microsoft in a garage, because the core models are owned by a handful of incumbents. Instead, new companies are born already dependent on the pricing, terms, and permissions of the model providers. As with containerization, the economic shock is immediate: a competitive free-for-all where margins collapse and only oligopolies survive.</p>
<p data-start="1435" data-end="2061">The consequences mirror what users experience under opaque moderation. Just as a Facebook ban removes an individual’s agency without recourse, chokepoint capitalism removes entrepreneurial agency by foreclosing routes of innovation. Value flows through a few bottlenecks—APIs, cloud platforms, ad networks—and is captured there. Downstream firms may benefit by integrating cheaper knowledge services into healthcare, education, or finance, just as Walmart leveraged logistics to dominate retail. Consumers may benefit from lower costs. But innovators are squeezed, and workers find themselves displaced into thinner margins.</p>
<p data-start="2063" data-end="2528">In this sense, the economic enclosure and the social enclosure are inseparable. The same infrastructures that govern who can speak, trade, or participate are those that determine where profits accumulate. Chokepoint capitalism is not a deviation from the gossip–religion–platform cycle; it is its culmination. What begins as an entropy-smoothing tool, stabilizing flows of communication or trade, ends as a system of rigid bottlenecks, both semantic and economic.</p>
