art="0" data-end="137">Here’s the draft <strong data-start="17" data-end="52">Continuity of Constraint Cycles</strong> section that ties together gossip, religion, platforms, and chokepoint capitalism:</p>
<hr data-start="139" data-end="142">
<h3 data-start="144" data-end="181">Continuity of Constraint Cycles</h3>
<p data-start="183" data-end="445">When viewed together, the four stages of semantic infrastructure—gossip, religion, platforms, and chokepoint capitalism—form a repeating cycle. Each emerges as a negentropic innovation that stabilizes cooperation, only to harden into a mechanism of constraint.</p>
<p data-start="447" data-end="719"><strong data-start="447" data-end="457">Gossip</strong> originated as a local memory system, compressing the complexities of individual behavior into simple, transmissible stories. It enabled trust and social cohesion in small groups, but once reputations ossified, there was no appeal: a rumor could define a life.</p>
<p data-start="721" data-end="1039"><strong data-start="721" data-end="733">Religion</strong> scaled this compression to entire civilizations. By codifying cosmologies and moral codes, it provided durable meaning across generations. Yet stability was purchased at the cost of hierarchy and dogma, with dissent often criminalized. What began as shared semantic scaffolding became coercive doctrine.</p>
<p data-start="1041" data-end="1472"><strong data-start="1041" data-end="1054">Platforms</strong> are the digital heirs to these infrastructures. Promising frictionless connection, they centralized communication and commerce at global scale. But the same mechanisms that smoothed interaction—feeds, metrics, recommendation systems—matured into enclosures. Behavior was funneled into monetizable forms, and opaque moderation became the algorithmic equivalent of gossip and dogma: judgments issued without recourse.</p>
<p data-start="1474" data-end="1979"><strong data-start="1474" data-end="1499">Chokepoint capitalism</strong> represents the culmination of this cycle. Just as containerization transformed trade while consolidating profits downstream, generative infrastructures reshape knowledge and services while reinforcing oligopoly. Value accrues not to innovators but to incumbents and consumers, while entrepreneurs and workers find themselves trapped in narrowing margins. The chokepoint captures both semantic authority (who may speak, publish, participate) and economic rents (who may profit).</p>
<p data-start="1981" data-end="2418">This continuity reveals a paradox. Every stage begins as an answer to entropy: gossip stabilizes small-group trust, religion provides shared meaning, platforms lower the cost of communication, and chokepoint infrastructures promise efficiency. Yet each eventually constrains what it sought to free, producing new forms of enclosure. The cycle repeats because entropy-smoothing mechanisms are attractive but inherently prone to capture.</p>
