</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>*{Introduction}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Recurring cultural anxiety: computers as black boxes that “think” or judge.
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Contrast between 1950s optimism and modern dystopian experiences (e.g., opaque Facebook bans).
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Thesis: semantic infrastructures (gossip, religion, platforms, chokepoint capitalism) emerge to stabilize meaning, then ossify into constraints.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{The Early Frame (1950s Textbooks)}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Automatic computers as deterministic tools requiring extensive preprocessing.
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Human labor behind “automatic” outputs.
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Early debate: Berkeley’s </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{Giant Brains} vs. Turing’s “Can a Machine Think?”
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Paradox of over-ascribing intelligence to machines.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Cultural Imaginaries (1957–1962)}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{Desk Set} (1957): EMERAC as clerical threat, but limited in judgment.
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{The Creation of the Humanoids} (1962): robots as existential authority, defining humanity.
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Shared anxieties: are machines tools, partners, or judges?
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Platforms as Enclosures}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Shift from institutional scarcity to ubiquitous global infrastructures.
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> The illusion of openness vs. reality of opaque governance.
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Case study: Facebook bans as “Black Mirror bureaucracy.”
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Platforms as gossip/religion at planetary scale: compress, codify, constrain.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Chokepoint Capitalism}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Neumann’s containerization analogy.
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Microprocessor vs. containerization: invention vs. commodification.
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Generative systems as containerization: incumbents reinforced, innovators squeezed.
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Consumers benefit, but workers and entrepreneurs hollowed out.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Continuity of Constraint Cycles}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Gossip: local trust </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rightarrow</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> reputation traps.
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Religion: shared meaning </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rightarrow</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> dogma.
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Platforms: communication </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rightarrow</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> enshittified chokepoints.
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Chokepoint capitalism: efficiency </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rightarrow</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> oligopoly and commodification.
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> General cycle: negentropic innovation becomes constraint.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{RSVP and Semantic Infrastructures}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> RSVP lens: </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> compressed, </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vec</span></span><span>{v}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> bottlenecked, </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> offloaded.
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Toward a fourth stage: entropy-aware semantic infrastructures.
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Principles: entropic balancing, ephemeralization, recursive accountability, semantic watermarking.
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Aim: metabolize capture, recycling it into commons rather than rent extraction.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>*{Conclusion}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> The debate hasn’t changed (“can machines think?” </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rightarrow</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> “can machines judge?” </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rightarrow</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> “can machines make us rich?”).
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Dependence has changed: from rare machines to total infrastructures.
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Without new design principles, cycles of capture repeat.
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> RSVP offers a speculative path: stabilize meaning without ossification.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>*{Keywords}
semantic infrastructures; gossip; religion; platforms; chokepoint capitalism; containerization; generative systems; entropy; capture; Relativistic Scalar Vector Plenum (RSVP)
