c-16-25.333-24-45-24-59z"></path></svg></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> encodes conversational/artistic transport across contexts.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2241" data-end="2371">
<p data-start="2243" data-end="2371">Entropy <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.05764em;">S</span></span></span></span> is the magnitude of unresolved obstruction classes (contradictions, ambiguities) that remain live in the system.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="2373" data-end="2376">
<h4 data-start="2378" data-end="2396">Conclusion.</h4>
<p data-start="2397" data-end="2783">Thus, both conversations and artworks are equivalent to <strong data-start="2453" data-end="2490">context-trained generative models</strong>: they compress a corpus into a functorial structure, generate outputs as sections or endomorphisms, and metabolize entropy through higher morphisms. Infrastructures differ only in how they treat obstructions—whether as noise to erase, judgments to enforce, or higher-order data to preserve.</p>
<p data-start="2785" data-end="2798"><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.675em;"></span><span class="mord amsrm">□</span></span></span></span></p>
