n-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="87">Here’s the corollary phrased so it flows directly from the theorem we framed earlier:</p>
<hr data-start="89" data-end="92">
<h3 data-start="94" data-end="148">Corollary (Generative Cinema as Chokepoint Bot).</h3>
<p data-start="149" data-end="283">Generative movie systems demonstrate the collapse of contextual generativity into chokepoints when semantic obstructions are erased.</p>
<hr data-start="285" data-end="288">
<h4 data-start="290" data-end="310">Proof Sketch.</h4>
<ol data-start="312" data-end="1356">
<li data-start="312" data-end="647">
<p data-start="315" data-end="647"><strong data-start="315" data-end="338">As contextual bots.</strong><br data-start="338" data-end="341">
A generative movie application is a bot trained on cinematic context: its corpus is the archive of tropes, its inference mechanism is relevance activation, and its outputs are trajectories rendered as shots. By the theorem, this qualifies as a context-trained functor: corpus → model → generative output.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="649" data-end="965">
<p data-start="652" data-end="965"><strong data-start="652" data-end="685">Semantic infrastructure case.</strong><br data-start="685" data-end="688">
If the application preserves contradictions between genres, interpretations, and stylistic traditions, its generativity functions like Shakespeare: obstructions are visible, contested, and metabolized. Each new film is a section of the presheaf that respects local ambiguity.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="967" data-end="1356">
<p data-start="970" data-end="1356"><strong data-start="970" data-end="1001">Chokepoint capitalism case.</strong><br data-start="1001" data-end="1004">
If instead the system suppresses these obstructions in favor of engagement or market optimization, it collapses into Netflix-like recommendation cinema. Tropes are narrowed to pre-approved templates, camera paths are optimized for metrics, and provenance is hidden. The output is still a bot, but now trained on the chokepoint rather than the corpus.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<hr data-start="1358" data-end="1361">
<h4 data-start="1363" data-end="1397">Interpretation (RSVP lens).</h4>
<ul data-start="1398" data-end="1540">
<li data-start="1398" data-end="1442">
<p data-start="1400" data-end="1442"><strong data-start="1400" data-end="1405">Φ</strong>: reduced to engagement embeddings.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1443" data-end="1495">
<p data-start="1445" data-end="1495"><strong data-start="1445" data-end="1451">𝒗</strong>: funneled through recommendation funnels.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1496" data-end="1540">
<p data-start="1498" data-end="1540"><strong data-start="1498" data-end="1503">S</strong>: expelled rather than metabolized.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="1542" data-end="1704">Thus, generative cinema is a worked example of the corollary: <strong data-start="1604" data-end="1702">when infrastructures erase obstructions, context-trained bots degenerate into chokepoint bots.</strong></p>
<p data-start="1706" data-end="1719"><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.675em;"></span><span class="mord amsrm">□</span></span></span></span></p>
<hr data-start="1721" data-end="1724">
