ay a bookend effect:</p>
<hr data-start="109" data-end="112">
<h3 data-start="114" data-end="128">Abstract</h3>
<p data-start="129" data-end="635">Since the 1950s, debates about whether machines can “think” have obscured a deeper cycle: infrastructures built to stabilize meaning inevitably risk hardening into chokepoints. Early textbooks on automatic computers emphasized the hidden human labor behind outputs; mid-century films like <em data-start="418" data-end="428">Desk Set</em> and <em data-start="433" data-end="464">The Creation of the Humanoids</em> staged anxieties about machines as partners or judges; today’s platforms enforce opaque bans and deliver algorithmic recommendations that collapse context into funnels.</p>
<p data-start="637" data-end="1389">This essay argues that every conversation and artwork is already a <strong data-start="704" data-end="734">bot trained on its context</strong>. What matters is not the generative act itself but the infrastructure in which it is embedded. Semantic infrastructures metabolize contradictions as living fuel, while chokepoint capitalism suppresses them, producing generators trained on constraint rather than context. Through historical examples, cultural imaginaries, and economic analogies (containerization, generative models), the essay shows how this cycle has repeated across domains. RSVP theory offers a field-theoretic frame for imagining infrastructures that preserve scalar density (<span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord">Φ</span></span></span></span>), keep vector flows (<span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>⃗</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{v}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.714em;"></span><span class="mord accent"><span class="vlist-t"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.714em;"><span style="top: -3em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 3em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.03588em;">v</span></span><span style="top: -3em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 3em;"></span><span class="accent-body" style="left: -0.2077em;"><span class="overlay" style="height: 0.714em; width: 0.471em;"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.o