ings</strong> that mirror your thesis and give the essay a rhythm around the RSVP/chokepoint contrast:</p>
<hr data-start="136" data-end="139">
<h2 data-start="141" data-end="171">Proposed Section Headings</h2>
<ol data-start="173" data-end="1615">
<li data-start="173" data-end="316">
<p data-start="176" data-end="316"><strong data-start="176" data-end="225">Introduction: From Black Boxes to Bottlenecks</strong><br data-start="225" data-end="228">
(sets up the recurring anxiety: machines as judges, infrastructures as chokepoints)</p>
</li>
<li data-start="318" data-end="460">
<p data-start="321" data-end="460"><strong data-start="321" data-end="373">Metabolizing Context (1950s Automatic Computers)</strong><br data-start="373" data-end="376">
(human labor behind machines; computers framed as extensions, not replacements)</p>
</li>
<li data-start="462" data-end="617">
<p data-start="465" data-end="617"><strong data-start="465" data-end="537">Ambiguity on Screen (<em data-start="488" data-end="498">Desk Set</em> and <em data-start="503" data-end="534">The Creation of the Humanoids</em>)</strong><br data-start="537" data-end="540">
(filmic imaginaries of machines as partners, assistants, or authorities)</p>
</li>
<li data-start="619" data-end="755">
<p data-start="622" data-end="755"><strong data-start="622" data-end="670">Expelling Contradiction (Platforms and Bans)</strong><br data-start="670" data-end="673">
(Facebook, algorithms, opaque judgments—machines as Black Mirror bureaucracy)</p>
</li>
<li data-start="757" data-end="905">
<p data-start="760" data-end="905"><strong data-start="760" data-end="807">From Containerization to Generative Systems</strong><br data-start="807" data-end="810">
(Neumann’s analogy: microprocessors as open experimentation vs. containers as chokepoints)</p>
</li>
<li data-start="907" data-end="1043">
<p data-start="910" data-end="1043"><strong data-start="910" data-end="934">Cycles of Constraint</strong><br data-start="934" data-end="937">
(gossip → religion → platforms → chokepoint capitalism; each begins as negentropic, ends as ossified)</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1045" data-end="1180">
<p data-start="1048" data-end="1180"><strong data-start="1048" data-end="1093">Semantic Infrastructure as an Alternative</strong><br data-start="1093" data-end="1096">
(RSVP lens: Φ preserved, 𝒗 distributed, S metabolized; category/sheaf framing)</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1182" data-end="1344">
<p data-start="1185" data-end="1344"><strong data-start="1185" data-end="1234">Conversations and Artworks as Contextual Bots</strong><br data-start="1234" data-end="1237">
(formal theorem: every act of expression is a bot trained on its corpus; corollary on chokepoint bots)</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1346" data-end="1475">
<p data-start="1349" data-end="1475"><strong data-start="1349" data-end="1384">Generative Cinema as Case Study</strong><br data-start="1384" data-end="1387">
(trope activation, camera trajectories, Shakespeare vs. Netflix, chokepoint cinema)</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1477" data-end="1615">
<p data-start="1481" data-end="1615"><strong data-start="1481" data-end="1520">Conclusion: Keeping the Plenum Open</strong><br data-start="1520" data-end="1523">
(RSVP as design grammar: systems that metabolize contradiction instead of expelling it)</p>
</li>
</ol>
<hr data-start="1617" data-end="1620">
