x w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[3px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="100">Here’s how you could frame this new DeepMind paper inside the larger essay you’ve been developing:</p>
<hr data-start="102" data-end="105">
<h2 data-start="107" data-end="165">Appendix C: Theoretical Limits of Embedding Retrieval</h2>
<p data-start="167" data-end="603">A recent paper from Google DeepMind (<em data-start="204" data-end="265">On the Theoretical Limitations of Embedding-Based Retrieval</em>, Weller et al. 2025) has direct bearing on the chokepoint/semantic infrastructure distinction. The authors prove, using results from communication complexity and sign-rank theory, that <strong data-start="451" data-end="558">single-vector embeddings have a hard mathematical ceiling on what they can represent in retrieval tasks</strong>, no matter how large the model or dataset.</p>
<h3 data-start="605" data-end="639">C.1 The Sign-Rank Constraint</h3>
<p data-start="640" data-end="1012">For an embedding model of dimension <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6944em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">d</span></span></span></span>, there exists a maximum number of query–document relationships it can reliably separate. This bound is polynomial in <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6944em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">d</span></span></span></span>, but the space of possible query–document combinations grows combinatorially in the number of documents <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.4306em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">n</span></span></span></span>. Thus, as <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n \to \infty</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.4306em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">n</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">→</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.4306em;"></span><span class="mord">∞</span></span></span></span>, <strong data-start="943" data-end="1009">no single-vector embedding can capture all relevance relations</strong>.</p>
<h3 data-start="1014" data-end="1043">C.2 The LIMIT Benchmark</h3>
<p data-start="1044" data-end="1126">To stress-test this theoretical limit, the authors introduced the LIMIT dataset:</p>
<ul data-start="1127" data-end="1364">
<li data-start="1127" data-end="1207">
<p data-start="1129" data-end="1207">Documents are synthetic “profiles” (e.g., <em data-start="1171" data-end="1203">Alice likes Apples and Quokkas</em>).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1208" data-end="1291">
<p data-start="1210" data-end="1291">Queries ask for all documents matching a property (e.g., <em data-start="1267" data-end="1287">Who likes Quokkas?</em>).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1292" data-end="1364">
<p data-start="1294" data-end="1364">Evaluation requires distinguishing every possible pair of documents.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="1366" data-end="1633">State-of-the-art embedding models, including Gemini and GritLM, fail catastrophically (recall@100 below 20%). Meanwhile, a decades-old sparse method (BM25) performs well, because its effectively infinite-dimensional representation can handle the combinatorial load.</p>
<h3 data-start="1635" data-end="1685">C.3 Implications for Semantic Infrastructure</h3>
<p data-start="1686" data-end="1754">This result reframes the chokepoint problem in mathematical terms:</p>
<ul data-start="1755" data-end="2216">
<li data-start="1755" data-end="2014">
<p data-start="1757" data-end="2014"><strong data-start="1757" data-end="1785">Single-vector embeddings</strong> are themselves <strong data-start="1801" data-end="1831">chokepoint infrastructures</strong> — they compress <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord">Φ</span></span></span></span> into a fixed <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6944em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">d</span></span></span></span>-dimensional space, bottleneck <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>⃗</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{v}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.714em;"></span><span class="mord accent"><span class="vlist-t"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.714em;"><span style="top: -3em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 3em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.03588em;">v</span></span><span style="top: -3em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 3em;"></span><span class="accent-body" style="left: -0.2077em;"><span class="overlay" style="height: 0.714em; width: 0.471em;"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="0.471em" height="0.714em" viewBox="0 0 471 714" preserveAspectRatio="xMinYM