</li>
<li data-start="1264" data-end="1783">
<p data-start="1266" data-end="1783"><strong data-start="1266" data-end="1307">Multi-Vector Models as Sheaf Patches.</strong><br data-start="1307" data-end="1310">
Multi-vector models (e.g., ColBERT and successors) approximate the behavior of sheaves: instead of collapsing a document into one global section, they preserve multiple local sections that can be glued under MaxSim. This allows finer-grained preservation of overlaps, exactly the obstruction we saw in the 4×4 tetrahedron toy case. In RSVP terms, the field retains local semantic density rather than over-compressing <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord">Φ</span></span></span></span>, distributing negentropic flow more evenly.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1785" data-end="2396">
<p data-start="1787" data-end="2396"><strong data-start="1787" data-end="1835">Sparse Models as Infinite-Dimensional Plena.</strong><br data-start="1835" data-end="1838">
Sparse methods (BM25, SPLADE, etc.) avoid collapse by working in extremely high-dimensional spaces, effectively taking the embedding dimension <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\to \infty</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6944em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">d</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">→</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.4306em;"></span><span class="mord">∞</span></span></span></span>. This guarantees that many combinatorial labelings remain linearly separable, but sacrifices compactness and semantic smoothness. From an RSVP perspective, sparse models preserve raw entropy <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.05764em;">S</span></span></span></span> rather than metabolizing it, functioning as archives of lexical microstates. They excel where surface structure dominates, but falter in contexts requiring recursive inference or entropic smoothing.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="2398" data-end="2431">In category-theoretic language:</p>
<ul data-start="2432" data-end="2883">
<li data-start="2432" data-end="2576">
<p data-start="2434" data-end="2576"><strong data-start="2434" data-end="2458">Single-vector models</strong> enforce a premature colimit: all local sections are glued into one global vector, obliterating nontrivial overlaps.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2577" data-end="2683">
<p data-start="2579" data-end="2683"><strong data-start="2579" data-end="2602">Multi-vector models</strong> allow a homotopy colimit: overlaps are preserved via multiple representatives.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2684" data-end="2784">
<p data-start="2686" data-end="2784"><strong data-start="2686" data-end="2704">Cross-encoders</strong> recompute fiber products directly, bypassing the need for global compression.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2785" data-end="2883">
<p data-start="2787" data-end="2883"><strong data-start="2787" data-end="2804">Sparse models</strong> enlarge the base category until almost any sheaf becomes trivially glueable.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="2885" data-end="3330">From the RSVP vantage point, none of these is a final solution. Each represents a tradeoff between preserving semantic density (<span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord">Φ</span></span></span></span>), sustaining distributed flows (<span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>⃗</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{v}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.714em;"></span><span class="mord accent"><span class="vlist-t"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.714em;"><span style="top: -3em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 3em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.03588em;">v</span></span><span style="top: -3em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 3em;"></span><span class="accent-body" style="left: -0.2077em;"><span class="overlay" style="height: 0.714em; width: 0.471em;"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="0.471em" height="0.714em" viewBox="0 0 471 714" preserveAspectRatio="xMinYMin" style="width: 0.471em;"><path d="M377 20c0-5.333 1.833-10 5.5-14S391 0 397 0c4.667 0 8.667 1.667 12 5
