c-16-25.333-24-45-24-59z"></path></svg></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> (vector flow):</strong> bottlenecked into one similarity channel, losing the ability to differentiate trajectories of meaning.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1663" data-end="1779">
<p data-start="1665" data-end="1779"><strong data-start="1665" data-end="1697"><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.05764em;">S</span></span></span></span> (entropy/obstruction):</strong> expelled as noise or error rather than incorporated into the retrieval process.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="1781" data-end="1934">In practice, single-vector embeddings enforce low entropy by construction, but at the cost of suppressing the richness of possible relevance relations.</p>
<h3 data-start="1936" data-end="1985">7.3 Adaptive Colimits as Retrieval Strategy</h3>
<p data-start="1986" data-end="2303">We propose that retrieval should instead be modeled as an <strong data-start="2044" data-end="2064">adaptive colimit</strong> across multiple retrieval architectures. For each query context, the system measures the <em data-start="2154" data-end="2172">obstruction load</em>—the degree to which low-rank embeddings fail to preserve local consistency—and then escalates retrieval fidelity only as needed.</p>
<ul data-start="2304" data-end="2524">
<li data-start="2304" data-end="2361">
<p data-start="2306" data-end="2361">If obstruction is low, single-vector methods suffice.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2362" data-end="2434">
<p data-start="2364" data-end="2434">If moderate, multi-vector or sparse methods preserve local overlaps.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2435" data-end="2524">
<p data-start="2437" data-end="2524">If high, cross-encoder recomputation is selectively applied to the offending regions.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="2526" data-end="2895">Formally, the retrieval output becomes a homotopy colimit of partial results, weighted by a controller <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.4306em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.03588em;">π</span></span></span></span> that balances retrieval accuracy, computational cost, and entropy preservation. This ensures that local contradictions are either maintained (via sparse/multi-vector structures) or explicitly rewoven (via cross-encoders), rather than being silently erased.</p>
<h3 data-start="2897" data-end="2942">7.4 Semantic Infrastructure Perspective</h3>
<p data-start="2943" data-end="3449">This strategy exemplifies what we mean by <strong data-start="2985" data-end="3012">semantic infrastructure</strong>: retrieval systems designed not only to deliver results but also to maintain the structural integrity of meaning across scales. By treating obstructions as signals rather than errors, an RSVP retrieval model metabolizes semantic entropy into adaptive routing policies. In categorical terms, it resists premature global sections and instead organizes retrieval as a dynamic balance between compression, preservation, and recomputation.</p>
<h3 data-start="3451" data-end="3473">7.5 Implications</h3>
<p data-start="3474" data-end="3912">Such an approach reframes the role of retrieval models within chokepoint capitalism. Instead of reinforcing centralization through rigid embeddings that collapse diversity into proprietary bottlenecks, RSVP retrieval enables plural and adaptive infrastructures. In effect, it builds in a resistance to semantic capture: value is not solely concentrated at the compression layer but distributed across a spectrum of retrieval modalities.</p>
<hr data-start="3914" data-end="3917">
<p data-start="3919" data-end="4204" data-is-last-node="" data-is-only-node="">Would you like me to also draft a <strong data-start="3953" data-end="3997">figure caption–style diagram description</strong> (in prose, not visuals, since you prefer no diagrams) that walks the reader through how this adaptive routing works step by step? That could make the section more tangible without requiring an actual chart.</p></div></div></div></div><div class="flex min-h-[46px] justify-start"><div class="touch:-me-2 touch:-ms-3.5 -ms-2.5 -me-1 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-y-4 p-1 select-none touch:w-[calc(100%+--spacing(3.5))] -mt-1 w-[calc(100%+--spacing(2.5))] duration-[1.5s] focus-within:transition-none hover:transition-none pointer-events-none [mask-image:linear-gradient(to_right,black_33%,transparent_66%)] [mask-size:300%_100%] [mask-position:100%_0%] motion-safe:transition-[mask-position] group-hover/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-hover/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] group-focus-within/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-focus-within/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] has-data-[state=open]:pointer-events-auto has-data-[state=open]:[mask-position:0_0]" style="mask-position: 0% 0%;"><button class="text-token-text-secondary hover:bg-token-bg-secondary rounded-lg" aria-label="Copy" aria-pressed="false" data-testid="copy-turn-action-button" data-state="closed"><span class="flex items-center justify-center touch:w-10 h-8 w-8"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="currentColor" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="icon"><path d="M12.668 10.667C12.668 9.95614 12.668 9.46258 12.6367 9.0791C12.6137 8.79732 12.5758 8.60761 12.5244 8.46387L12.4688 8.33399C12.3148 8.03193 12.0803 7.77885 11.793 7.60254L11.666 7.53125C11.508 7.45087 11.2963 7.39395 10.9209 7.36328C10.5374 7.33197 10.0439 7.33203 9.33301 7.33203H6.5C5.78896 7.33203 5.29563 7.33195 4.91211 7.36328C4.63016 7.38632 4.44065 7.42413 4.29688 7.47559L4.16699 7.53125C3.86488 7.68518 3.61186 