" data-end="197">Here’s a prose <strong data-start="15" data-end="53">“figure caption” style walkthrough</strong> that could sit either inside the section or as a note below it, making the adaptive colimit idea more tangible without resorting to graphics:</p>
<hr data-start="199" data-end="202">
<p data-start="204" data-end="1376"><strong data-start="204" data-end="262">Figure (conceptual): RSVP Adaptive Retrieval Workflow.</strong><br data-start="262" data-end="265">
Imagine a query enters the system. In the first pass, it is routed through a <strong data-start="342" data-end="375">single-vector embedding model</strong>, producing a cheap, compressed retrieval list. At this point, the system measures the <em data-start="462" data-end="480">obstruction load</em>—a proxy for how much semantic contradiction or overlap has been lost in compression. If the obstruction is <strong data-start="588" data-end="595" data-is-only-node="">low</strong>, the system stops here, returning results quickly. If the obstruction is <strong data-start="669" data-end="681">moderate</strong>, the system activates a <strong data-start="706" data-end="738">multi-vector or sparse model</strong>, which distributes meaning across multiple channels or dimensions to recover overlaps. If the obstruction is <strong data-start="848" data-end="856">high</strong>, the system escalates selectively to a <strong data-start="896" data-end="913">cross-encoder</strong>, which recomputes exact pairwise relevance on only the ambiguous slice of documents. The outputs from these heterogeneous routes are then “glued” together via a <strong data-start="1075" data-end="1095">homotopy colimit</strong>, ensuring that local consistency conditions are respected and contradictions are either preserved (as overlaps) or explicitly resolved. The end result is a retrieval output that adapts its semantic fidelity to the demands of the query, rather than erasing complexity by default.</p>
<hr data-start="1378" data-end="1381">
<p data-start="1383" data-end="1596" data-is-last-node="" data-is-only-node="">Would you like me to also prepare a <strong data-start="1419" data-end="1457">short boxed “algorithm” pseudocode</strong> version, something that reads like an outline of steps 1–5, so readers who think procedurally can immediately see how the system executes?</p></div></div></div></div><div class="flex min-h-[46px] justify-start"><div class="touch:-me-2 touch:-ms-3.5 -ms-2.5 -me-1 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-y-4 p-1 select-none touch:w-[calc(100%+--spacing(3.5))] -mt-1 w-[calc(100%+--spacing(2.5))] duration-[1.5s] focus-within:transition-none hover:transition-none pointer-events-none [mask-image:linear-gradient(to_right,black_33%,transparent_66%)] [mask-size:300%_100%] [mask-position:100%_0%] motion-safe:transition-[mask-position] group-hover/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-hover/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] group-focus-within/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-focus-within/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] has-data-[state=open]:pointer-events-auto has-data-[state=open]:[mask-position:0_0]" style="mask-position: 0% 0%;"><button class="text-token-text-secondary hover:bg-token-bg-secondary rounded-lg" aria-label="Copy" aria-pressed="false" data-testid="copy-turn-action-button" data-state="closed"><span class="flex items-center justify-center touch:w-10 h-8 w-8"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="currentColor" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="icon"><path d="M12.668 10.667C12.668 9.95614 12.668 9.46258 12.6367 9.0791C12.6137 8.79732 12.5758 8.60761 12.5244 8.46387L12.4688 8.33399C12.3148 8.03193 12.0803 7.77885 11.793 7.60254L11.666 7.53125C11.508 7.45087 11.2963 7.39395 10.9209 7.36328C10.5374 7.33197 10.0439 7.33203 9.33301 7.33203H6.5C5.78896 7.33203 5.29563 7.33195 4.91211 7.36328C4.63016 7.38632 4.44065 7.42413 4.29688 7.47559L4.16699 7.53125C3.86488 7.68518 3.61186 7.9196 3.43555 8.20703L3.36524 8.33399C3.28478 8.49198 3.22795 8.70352 3